These instructions assume Linux.  Should be easily implemented on anything similar
running Python and systemd.

*** client install ***

Daemon need to run as root, or a user that can access the Postfix spool queues.

Copy queue_counts.py to /usr/local/bin (or elsewhere, and modify the service file)

$ chmod 0755 /usr/local/bin/queue_counts.py

Add the following line to /etc/services

postchecker     7678/tcp                        # PostChecker port

Copy queue_counts.service to /etc/systemd/system and run:

$ systemctl daemon-reload
$ systemctl enable queue_counts
$ systemctl start queue_counts

Assuming everything is OK,

$ systemctl status queue_counts

Should return good news.

Finally, the critical system test.  From an allowed IP address:

# nc <client name> 7678

If you see something like this, you're good to go:

[root@localhost main]# nc <client name> postchecker
Trying 127.0.0.1...
Connected to localhost (127.0.0.1).
Escape character is '^]'.
active:5
deferred:5
corrupt:0
hold:0
Connection closed by foreign host.
[root@localhost main]#

If you don't see something similar to that, check your log files for errors.
